484e-05], [" ", -11.502823829650879]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and community discussions regarding the Intel Graphics Compiler (IGC) and the Linux Kernel drivers for Intel Arc GPUs, there is **no official, standardized parameter named `ccs_mode`** that accepts values 1, 2, and 4 in the public driver interface.\n\nHowever, this terminology likely refers to one of three specific contexts. Below is the breakdown of the most probable interpretations and the correct technical distinctions for the Intel Arc A770 under the **Xe driver** (which usually refers to the **Intel Graphics Compiler (IGC)** or the **Xe Kernel Driver** in Linux).\n\n### Interpretation 1: **Compute Unit (CU) / Thread Group Mode in the Shader Compiler**\nIn the context of **Intel Graphics Compiler (IGC)** or **OneAPI/SYCL**, `ccs_mode` is not a standard flag, but users often confuse it with **`-ccs`** (Compute Capability Selection) or **thread group modes**.\n\nIf you are referring to **Compute Capability Selection** (`-ccs`):\n- **Mode 1**: Targets **Gen4** (older integrated graphics, e.g., 4th gen).\n- **Mode 2**: Targets **Gen8** (6th gen, e.g., Skylake).\n- **Mode 4**: Targets **Gen12** (12th gen, e.g., **Intel Arc A770**).\n\n\u2705 **Correct Usage for A770:** You would use **`-ccs 4`** (or Gen12) because the Intel Arc A770 is based on the **Xe2 architecture**, which falls under **Gen12** compute capability. Using mode 1 or 2 would generate suboptimal or incorrect code for the A770.\n\n---\n\n### Interpret"}